alex: heads up — customer hitting duplicate POSTs on retries after we moved to node 20 + undici 5.x. They see aborted fetches and then a second request that actually goes through.

jess: ugh. do we have a minimal repro or logs? any SDK version info? :thinking:

alex: repro attached below. stack shows `FetchError: request aborted` then retry succeeded. SDK: redwood-js-sdk@1.11.0, undici@5.11.0, node v20.4.0. Repro code (TS):

alex: ```ts
// simplified: reuse controller by mistake
const controller = new AbortController();
async function call() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal, method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    // backoff then retry, reusing same controller -> signal already aborted
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 200 * attempt));
    return fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal, method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
  }
}
```

paul: that definitely looks like signal reuse. If controller.abort() was called on first attempt, subsequent attempt will immediately throw. But you said the second POST still sometimes goes through?

alex: log shows: `request aborted` then a second HTTP line in our gateway access logs for the same operation id — sometimes with truncated body. Looks like race between abort and request start, maybe undici spawns a new stream for the second fetch but body got partially sent.

devex: couple angles: 1) ensure new AbortController per attempt (simplest). 2) check library retry layer — our SDK had a bug in 1.11.0 where we reused the upstream signal when wrapping fetch in retry helper. We fixed it in 1.11.2 by cloning behavior (new controller per try). Can you try pinned upgrade to redwood-js-sdk@1.11.2?

jess: also check undici/node mismatch: node core fetch landed behaviors changed across v20.x — undici interactions can be subtle. Upgrading undici to 5.13+ helped another customer.

alex: trying upgrade now. quick question: is there a recommended backoff pattern for SDK users to avoid accidental signal leaks?

devex: recommended pattern: create a fresh AbortController for each attempt and only forward a top-level timeout if you want a global cap: e.g.
```ts
const overall = new AbortController(); // global cap for the whole operation
for (let i=0;i<retries;i++){
  const attemptCtl = new AbortController();
  const signal = mergeSignals(overall.signal, attemptCtl.signal); // small helper that races signals
  try { await fetch(url, { signal }); break; } catch(e) { attemptCtl.abort(); /* cleanup */ }
}
```
If you can't merge, at least new controller per attempt.

paul: noted. Also log the `x-retry-count` and request ids so we can correlate with gateway. If we still see duplicate full bodies after this change, capture pcap or gateway trace.

alex: upgraded SDK to 1.11.2 and undici to 5.13.0 in a canary — initial run looks clean, no immediate duplicate POSTs. Will monitor and post back. Thanks all :+1:

support-bot: Escalation updated: tag added `signal-reuse-bug`, priority medium, owner alex.
